WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Safe configuration parsing begins with safe loading.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:13.000
The program should define what happens when the file is missing, cannot be opened, is unreadable, is too large, or is only partially read.

3
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.140
Those cases should not be surprising side paths; they are part of the parser contract.

4
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.420
File size and line length limits protect parser assumptions.

5
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:28.920
A configuration file may be generated incorrectly, truncated, concatenated, or edited by hand.

6
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:35.820
Explicit limits keep memory use, buffer handling, and parse time under control before the data reaches deeper logic.

7
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:42.980
Unexpected binary data, embedded null bytes, or invalid text for the expected encoding should be handled predictably.

8
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.400
A byte sequence that is valid for one parser may be invalid for another.

9
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:54.540
The loader and parser should agree on whether configuration is treated as text, structured bytes, or a specific encoded format.

10
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.580
Partial reads should not become trusted configuration state.

11
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:11.320
If the loader cannot read the full configuration source, the program should avoid applying a half-loaded result unless the format explicitly supports incremental records and the parser can validate each record independently.

12
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.320
Permissions and location awareness also matter.

13
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:24.660
The parser should know which file it intended to load, which deployment layer provided it, and whether failure should stop startup, fall back to defaults, or keep a prior known-good configuration.

14
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.520
That behavior should be documented rather than guessed.
